1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,784
Every November, for 35 minutes, the
sky over South Korea goes quiet.

2
00:00:06,234 --> 00:00:07,785
No plane takes off.

3
00:00:07,785 --> 00:00:09,010
No plane lands.

4
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:14,562
Aircraft already in the air are
told to circle above 3 km, and wait.

5
00:00:15,012 --> 00:00:22,716
On November 19, 2026, airlines rescheduled
130 flights around this single half hour.

6
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:25,131
The reason isn't a holiday.

7
00:00:25,131 --> 00:00:26,732
It isn't an emergency.

8
00:00:26,732 --> 00:00:27,678
It's an exam.

9
00:00:28,128 --> 00:00:32,230
This is the story of why an entire
country pauses for one test.

10
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:36,006
And why that has less to do
with passion than with design.

11
00:00:36,006 --> 00:00:39,457
We'll look at what changes
on exam day, why the skies

12
00:00:39,457 --> 00:00:43,037
close, how it compares to
the SAT, the year Korea tried

13
00:00:43,037 --> 00:00:46,488
two exams instead of one,
and what this design costs.

14
00:00:46,938 --> 00:00:53,587
The exam is called the Suneung, short
for the College Scholastic Ability Test.

15
00:00:53,587 --> 00:00:57,594
This year, 551,864 people
signed up to take it.

16
00:00:58,044 --> 00:01:02,154
It begins at 8:40 a.m., and
runs until late afternoon.

17
00:01:02,154 --> 00:01:06,417
Korean, math, English, Korean
history, social or natural

18
00:01:06,417 --> 00:01:10,222
sciences, and an optional
second foreign language.

19
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:11,364
All on one day.

20
00:01:11,814 --> 00:01:15,564
And on that day, the country
adjusts its schedule around it.

21
00:01:15,564 --> 00:01:19,252
Government offices and many
companies push their start time

22
00:01:19,252 --> 00:01:22,815
back to 10 a.m., so the roads
stay clear for test takers.

23
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:25,878
Subways add extra trains
during the morning rush.

24
00:01:26,328 --> 00:01:29,080
Around test sites, traffic is restricted.

25
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:32,168
And if a student is
running late, a police car

26
00:01:32,168 --> 00:01:34,920
or motorcycle may carry them to the gate.

27
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:39,018
Outside the gates, younger
students and families gather

28
00:01:39,018 --> 00:01:42,866
before sunrise, holding signs
and handing out warm drinks.

29
00:01:42,866 --> 00:01:46,050
Then the gates close, and
everything goes quiet.

30
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,558
Inside, phones and
smartwatches are not allowed.

31
00:01:49,558 --> 00:01:52,233
Even the exam papers have their own story.

32
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:56,502
The people who write the questions
are kept in an isolated location

33
00:01:56,502 --> 00:02:00,324
for weeks, cut off from the
outside, until the test is over.

34
00:02:00,774 --> 00:02:04,496
Then, at 1:05 p.m., comes
the most protected part

35
00:02:04,496 --> 00:02:07,686
of the day: the English listening section.

36
00:02:08,136 --> 00:02:10,981
For 35 minutes, recorded
audio plays through

37
00:02:10,981 --> 00:02:13,438
classroom speakers across the country.

38
00:02:13,438 --> 00:02:15,119
Each recording plays once.

39
00:02:15,119 --> 00:02:18,028
One loud engine overhead,
and a student could

40
00:02:18,028 --> 00:02:20,808
miss a question they
will never hear again.

41
00:02:21,258 --> 00:02:22,679
So the skies close.

42
00:02:22,679 --> 00:02:24,100
Not just airliners.

43
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:28,362
Helicopters, light aircraft,
and drones are grounded too.

44
00:02:28,812 --> 00:02:31,849
Only emergency aircraft
are allowed to fly.

45
00:02:31,849 --> 00:02:35,028
Everything else waits,
until the audio stops.

46
00:02:35,478 --> 00:02:40,688
If you grew up in the United States, or
the U.K., this may be hard to picture.

47
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:43,560
So let's put it next
to something familiar.

48
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:44,094
The SAT.

49
00:02:44,544 --> 00:02:48,748
In the U.S., the SAT is offered
around 7 or 8 times a year.

50
00:02:48,748 --> 00:02:52,311
The digital version takes
a little over two hours.

51
00:02:52,311 --> 00:02:55,446
You can retake it, and
many colleges combine

52
00:02:55,446 --> 00:02:58,368
your best section scores across attempts.

53
00:02:58,818 --> 00:03:02,233
And an American college
application is a bundle.

54
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:05,363
Grades, essays, activities,
recommendations.

55
00:03:05,363 --> 00:03:08,706
Some colleges don't
require test scores at all.

56
00:03:09,156 --> 00:03:12,924
That bundle means no single
morning carries the whole weight.

57
00:03:12,924 --> 00:03:16,507
A bad test day can be balanced
by four years of grades, or

58
00:03:16,507 --> 00:03:20,028
simply by taking the test
again a couple of months later.

59
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:23,503
The Suneung starts from
the opposite design.

60
00:03:23,503 --> 00:03:24,121
One test.

61
00:03:24,121 --> 00:03:24,671
One day.

62
00:03:24,671 --> 00:03:30,102
One national score, that universities
across the country can read the same way.

63
00:03:30,552 --> 00:03:33,373
So the planes don't
stop because of passion.

64
00:03:33,373 --> 00:03:35,874
They stop because, if this one day goes

65
00:03:35,874 --> 00:03:38,760
wrong, there's no second
day to fall back on.

66
00:03:39,210 --> 00:03:42,268
And here's the part
most people don't know.

67
00:03:42,268 --> 00:03:44,900
Korea actually tried a second chance.

68
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:48,242
In 1993, the very first
Suneung was held twice.

69
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:50,874
Once in August, and once in November.

70
00:03:51,324 --> 00:03:56,267
It replaced an older national exam that
focused heavily on textbook knowledge.

71
00:03:56,267 --> 00:03:59,626
The new test was meant to
measure reasoning, which is

72
00:03:59,626 --> 00:04:02,604
where the name 'scholastic
ability' comes from.

73
00:04:03,054 --> 00:04:06,076
Students could use the
better of their two scores.

74
00:04:06,076 --> 00:04:07,043
It sounded fair.

75
00:04:07,043 --> 00:04:11,334
But the August exam turned out to be
much easier than the November one.

76
00:04:11,784 --> 00:04:14,881
And that broke the whole
point of a national score.

77
00:04:14,881 --> 00:04:19,800
If two tests aren't equally hard, the same
number no longer means the same thing.

78
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:23,872
So after a single year, the
two-exam plan was dropped.

79
00:04:23,872 --> 00:04:28,098
Since then, the Suneung has been
held once a year, in November.

80
00:04:28,548 --> 00:04:30,829
Notice what the designers chose here.

81
00:04:30,829 --> 00:04:33,665
They had a second chance,
and they gave it up.

82
00:04:33,665 --> 00:04:37,178
Not because second chances
are bad, but because, for this

83
00:04:37,178 --> 00:04:40,692
system, a fair comparison
mattered more than flexibility.

84
00:04:41,142 --> 00:04:43,407
Think about what one exam gives you.

85
00:04:43,407 --> 00:04:46,113
Everyone answers the
same questions, at the

86
00:04:46,113 --> 00:04:48,567
same minute, under the same conditions.

87
00:04:48,567 --> 00:04:51,462
That makes a national
score simple to compare.

88
00:04:51,912 --> 00:04:55,131
Weeks later, results
come back as a standard

89
00:04:55,131 --> 00:04:58,423
score, a percentile,
and a grade from 1 to 9.

90
00:04:58,423 --> 00:05:02,153
In most subjects, grade 1
roughly means the top 4%.

91
00:05:02,153 --> 00:05:05,445
In other words, your
result depends partly on

92
00:05:05,445 --> 00:05:08,664
how everyone else did,
on that same morning.

93
00:05:09,114 --> 00:05:13,906
A ranking like that only works if
everyone faced exactly the same test.

94
00:05:13,906 --> 00:05:17,618
Two different exam days would
mean two different tests.

95
00:05:17,618 --> 00:05:20,250
That's exactly what went wrong in 1993.

96
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:25,015
But it also means the conditions
themselves have to be protected.

97
00:05:25,015 --> 00:05:25,745
A late bus.

98
00:05:25,745 --> 00:05:26,740
A noisy street.

99
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:28,134
An airplane overhead.

100
00:05:28,134 --> 00:05:32,051
In a one-day system, each of
those stops being one person's

101
00:05:32,051 --> 00:05:35,436
bad luck, and becomes a
problem for the whole exam.

102
00:05:35,886 --> 00:05:40,426
To be fair, the Suneung isn't the
only way into a Korean university.

103
00:05:40,426 --> 00:05:45,366
Many seats are filled through school
records and other application routes.

104
00:05:45,816 --> 00:05:49,649
But many of those routes still
require a minimum Suneung grade.

105
00:05:49,649 --> 00:05:53,664
And for a large share of seats,
especially at the most competitive

106
00:05:53,664 --> 00:05:57,192
universities, the score is
still the main deciding factor.

107
00:05:57,642 --> 00:06:00,614
Put it together, and that's the scale.

108
00:06:00,614 --> 00:06:03,507
More than half a million test takers.

109
00:06:03,507 --> 00:06:04,915
130 flights moved.

110
00:06:04,915 --> 00:06:07,026
35 minutes of closed skies.

111
00:06:07,476 --> 00:06:09,481
Now, every system has a cost.

112
00:06:09,481 --> 00:06:13,284
And this one's cost is built
into the very same design.

113
00:06:13,734 --> 00:06:17,240
If a student gets sick,
panics, or simply has a bad

114
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:20,334
morning, the next chance
is a full year away.

115
00:06:20,784 --> 00:06:22,822
So many people take it again.

116
00:06:22,822 --> 00:06:27,319
This year, more than 170,000
applicants were repeat test-takers.

117
00:06:27,319 --> 00:06:30,552
That's the highest number
in over two decades.

118
00:06:31,002 --> 00:06:32,644
And they tend to do well.

119
00:06:32,644 --> 00:06:35,995
On last year's exam, about
2 out of 3 students with

120
00:06:35,995 --> 00:06:39,082
top average grades in
Korean, math, and inquiry

121
00:06:39,082 --> 00:06:42,498
subjects were graduates,
not current high schoolers.

122
00:06:42,948 --> 00:06:47,603
Around that second attempt, a whole
industry of retake academies has grown up,

123
00:06:47,603 --> 00:06:52,020
built for people who spend another
year preparing for the same single day.

124
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:57,479
And every year, outside the school gates,
families wait through the afternoon.

125
00:06:57,479 --> 00:07:01,782
For a test that lasts one day, but
can shape the years that follow.

126
00:07:02,232 --> 00:07:06,853
This isn't a question of more pressure
or less pressure than anywhere else.

127
00:07:06,853 --> 00:07:09,009
It's a different shape of pressure.

128
00:07:09,009 --> 00:07:12,828
Instead of being spread across
many dates and many parts of an

129
00:07:12,828 --> 00:07:16,032
application, it gathers on
one Thursday in November.

130
00:07:16,482 --> 00:07:19,027
Step back, and you can see the trade.

131
00:07:19,027 --> 00:07:23,290
The SAT approach spreads the
risk across many chances and many

132
00:07:23,290 --> 00:07:27,073
inputs, but the results are
harder to compare directly.

133
00:07:27,073 --> 00:07:30,924
The Suneung approach puts
everything into one comparable

134
00:07:30,924 --> 00:07:34,844
number, but concentrates all
of the risk on a single day.

135
00:07:34,844 --> 00:07:36,426
Neither choice is free.

136
00:07:36,876 --> 00:07:40,219
So when the planes stop
over Korea, it isn't really

137
00:07:40,219 --> 00:07:43,037
a show of how seriously
anyone takes exams.

138
00:07:43,037 --> 00:07:46,380
It's what a one-day system
needs, in order to work.

139
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:49,473
At 1:40, the listening section ends.

140
00:07:49,473 --> 00:07:50,647
The audio stops.

141
00:07:50,647 --> 00:07:55,566
And somewhere above the clouds,
the first plane begins its descent.

142
00:07:56,016 --> 00:07:57,616
What about where you live?

143
00:07:57,616 --> 00:07:59,523
Does anything stop for an exam?

144
00:07:59,523 --> 00:08:00,000
Tell me in the comments.

145
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,661
I'd genuinely like to know.

146
00:08:02,661 --> 00:08:05,430
And if you'd like to
see how other systems in

147
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:08,321
Korea work, from housing
to hagwons, subscribe.

148
00:08:08,321 --> 00:08:09,552
Thanks for watching.
